daemon: ar8030-lifecycled tuning re-apply and hook-script dispatch

Fills in the two gaps 0023's own patch called out as deliberately
deferred: bandwidth tuning across reconnects, and hook-script dispatch
on state transitions (so LED control and similar can react without a
resident poller).

Bandwidth, not bandwidth+channel: S65ar8030-transport-tx's own former
apply_link_tuning() only ever managed bandwidth, matching parity rather
than growing scope. Channel is additionally a hard no for now: this
project's own ar8030-linkctl found BB_GET_CHAN_INFO segfaults
deterministically on this device's 42-channel config, so there is no
safe way to read the chip's current channel at all -- and reading it is
exactly what detecting a live change would need.

The tuning design itself changed once during live testing. The first
version read the chip's current bandwidth periodically and persisted it
whenever it differed from what was last applied, meant to capture a
live `ar8030-linkctl bandwidth` change. Confirmed live that this is
unsafe: a fresh CONNECT can report a narrow bandwidth (e.g. 2.5M) for a
moment before the two sides finish negotiating up to what was actually
requested -- the same "bandwidth-plateau" behavior the old
apply_link_tuning() fought by re-issuing its own fixed value every 30s
-- and a mid-negotiation reading is indistinguishable from a genuine
operator change by value alone. It self-inflicted proof of that: it
persisted a transient 2MHz plateau reading over a real 20MHz default
within one second of connecting, which would have silently degraded
every future reconnect. Replaced with periodic re-assertion of the
intended value instead (BB_SET_BANDWIDTH is a fast, idempotent
override, safe to reissue on an already-correct link) -- simpler, and
matches the one behavior already proven to work rather than trying to
be clever about detecting intent from a noisy reading.

Hooks: settled on run-parts-style dispatch over udev (no existing
infrastructure, needs new kernel-side plumbing and a resident listener)
and status-file-plus-watchers (needs a resident poller per consumer --
recreates the exact "pile of watchdogs" problem this daemon exists to
fix) back when this was first designed. lc_hooks_dispatch() fork()+
exec()s every executable under <hook_dir>/<event>/ in lexical order
with argv[1]/env vars carrying the event, fire-and-forget (SIGCHLD
ignored at startup) so a slow or hung hook can never stall the
lifecycle thread. Wired into every real transition: "pairing" before a
(re-)pair attempt, "connected" on reaching CONNECT (both an
internally-triggered pair and an externally-observed one, e.g. the bind
button), "dropped" on a real disconnect, "idle" once for a unit that
has never been paired.

Confirmed live end-to-end (fresh reboot, uninterrupted): pairing/
connected hooks fired at the right transitions, bandwidth applied at
20MHz consistently on every check with the fix in place, and the link
settled into a stable, high-bitrate (18+ Mbit/s) stream sustained over
tens of minutes with zero further intervention.

